// ★★ TWO QUERIES, ONE `for` — and each row gets its OWN private return channel.
//
// A `for` may carry several rows joined by `&`, and the join is atomic: the body runs when
// EVERY row has its datum. When two of those rows are `!?` queries, the expansion hoists both
// requests under a SINGLE `new` with TWO binders:
//
//     new r1, r2 in {
//       @"fee"!(*r1, 500) | @"tax"!(*r2, 500) |
//       for(@fee <- r1 & @tax <- r2) { … }
//     }
//
// ★ WHY THIS IS NOT THE SAME BEAT AS 01. One return channel per query is a claim about
// PAIRING, and pairing is invisible when both services answer alike. If the two rows shared a
// return channel, either reply could satisfy either row — and a demo whose two desks both
// replied `50` would pass under that defect exactly as it passes without it.
//
// So the two desks answer DIFFERENTLY, from the SAME request argument:
//
//   desk      request   replies with   value
//   ────────  ────────  ─────────────  ─────
//   @"fee"    500       cents / 10     50
//   @"tax"    500       cents * 2      1000
//
// and the body publishes the two replies IN ROW ORDER, inside one list. The POSITIONS are the
// evidence: `[50, 1000, …]` says the fee row received the fee desk's reply. A shared channel
// would be free to produce `[1000, 50, …]`, and a demo that printed the two values on separate
// lines could not tell the two apart at all, because @"OUT" is a multiset and does not preserve
// the order things were published in.
//
// ★ THE THIRD ELEMENT IS COMPUTED FROM BOTH. `fee + tax` is summed by the reducer AFTER the
// join, so it exists only if BOTH round trips completed. `50`, `1000` and `1050` appear nowhere
// in the program.
//
// ★ ONE OBSERVATION, NOT THREE. The whole settlement is published as a single datum, so the
// transcript is byte-identical run to run. The alternative — three separate sends — would rest
// three data on a multiset channel and print them in whatever order the store held them.
//
// EXPECT: TWO observations — the control's greeting, and the settled list [50, 1000, 1050].

@"open"!("two desks, one join") |

for(@greeting <- @"open") {

  @"OUT"!(greeting) |

  for(@reply, @cents <- @"fee") { @reply!(cents / 10) } |
  for(@reply, @cents <- @"tax") { @reply!(cents * 2) } |

  for(@fee <- @"fee"!?(500) & @tax <- @"tax"!?(500)) {
    @"OUT"!([fee, tax, fee + tax])
  }
}
